    "description": "Employment and welfare in Iran connect access to paid work with social insurance, assistance, and support within families and communities. Research highlights uneven access to protection and important differences between headline labor-market performance and household security.",
    "tags": ["Employment", "Welfare", "Social insurance", "Gender", "Youth employment", "Household security"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:25:14Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:25:14Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Employment and Welfare in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore how jobs, gender, social insurance, assistance, and family support shape economic security in Iran."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "work-and-security",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How work connects to economic security",
        "content": "Employment and welfare belong together because earning an income and being protected against its loss are different dimensions of security. A study by Rezvan Ostadalidehaghi and Daniel Béland describes Iran’s welfare arrangements as involving public institutions, markets, families, and communities. It emphasizes uneven coverage and a fragmented institutional landscape. This framework helps explain why a household’s circumstances cannot be inferred from employment status alone. [s1](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/ijsw.70019)\n\nFor this guide, the subject divides into linked questions: how young people enter work, what shapes women’s employment, how informal work relates to protection, and how social security, retirement, and assistance support households. These branches require different evidence. A labor-market survey cannot by itself establish pension adequacy, while the existence of a welfare program does not demonstrate that all eligible households receive effective support.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "labor-market-recovery",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recovery can leave important gaps",
        "content": "The World Bank’s Spring/Summer 2023 Iran Economic Monitor places employment developments alongside economic growth and examines the pandemic’s gendered effects. Its assessment describes persistent difficulties for women, young people, and university graduates, together with underemployment. The report’s central relevance is that aggregate recovery can coexist with restricted opportunities for particular groups. Its findings concern the reporting periods covered by that edition, rather than verified conditions in October 2026. [s2](https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099107508212324041/pdf/IDU021ade6b80e3fd045190b8530b2ad462b0d6c.pdf)\n\nA separate World Bank research paper published in 2024 investigates women’s labor-force participation during the pandemic. The inspected publication abstract reports a decline and indicates that estimates differ when excess mortality is included in the analysis. This is a useful warning about interpretation: measured changes depend on the population, time period, and analytical specification. The abstract alone does not support a detailed account of every mechanism or subgroup. [s3](https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099754306122452987)",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "youth-transitions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Entering employment is part of entering adulthood",
        "content": "Youth employment also connects to housing and family formation. Historical research by Djavad Salehi-Isfahani examines difficult transitions from school to employment and marriage, including differences by family background and rural or urban residence. Its account shows why education, finding a first job, and establishing a household should be studied together. Because the paper was published in 2010, it supplies historical context rather than a current measure of young people’s prospects. [s4](https://www.belfercenter.org/sites/default/files/pantheon_files/files/publication/Salehi-Isfahani_DI-Working-Paper-3_Iran-Youth-Crisis.pdf)",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "welfare-arrangements",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Protection beyond wages",
        "content": "Ostadalidehaghi and Béland distinguish social insurance from assistance and support provided through families and communities. They argue that weak attachment to paid employment leaves many women dependent on family support and assistance rather than their own employment-linked protection. Their interpretation connects gender relations to the organization of welfare, rather than treating employment and social policy as separate subjects. [s1]\n\nThe authors also trace fragmentation to the coexistence of institutional structures shaped by Iran’s revolution and war. Their analysis is a scholarly interpretation, not an administrative register of current entitlements. A complete contemporary account must verify each program’s coverage, eligibility, financing, and benefit adequacy separately. Readers should therefore approach employment, retirement, and social support as connected but distinct subjects: the existence of work, a formal entitlement, and sufficient household income are different questions. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "Understanding Iran's welfare regime: The interplay of community, family, market, and state in a religious context",
        "publisher": "International Journal of Social Welfare, Wiley",
